==========================================================================================================================
C -- FAILURE MODE 3: SIGNIFICANCE WITH CORRELATED ERRORS.  L23 found 19.4 sigma -> 4.9 sigma in h9. Every sigma and every error bar in section A audited the same way.
==========================================================================================================================

C1  A8 -- 'the 13-15 sigma residual' against the framework's predicted zero
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    recomputed from the audit JSON at r = 1000 kpc, 12 clusters:
      median Newtonian M_dark/M_bar = 5.730   (A8 quotes 5.73)
      cluster-to-cluster rms        = 0.681  = 12% (A8 quotes '+/- 0.68, 12%')

    the framework residual: median 3.09 M_bar, cluster scatter 0.71
      quoted 15 sigma  =  3.09/(0.71/sqrt(12)) = 15.1  -- STATISTICS ONLY
    what is COHERENT across all twelve: one HSE estimator, one XMM calibration, one gas-density
    deprojection, one stellar-mass prescription, one kernel, one a0.  None of these averages down.
    a coherent X-ray mass calibration of only 10-15% moves the residual by a much larger fraction,
    because the residual is a DIFFERENCE of two large numbers:
      +/-5% coherent on M_HSE: residual 2.75 .. 3.43 M_bar   (fractional 11%)
      +/-10% coherent on M_HSE: residual 2.42 .. 3.76 M_bar   (fractional 22%)
      +/-15% coherent on M_HSE: residual 2.08 .. 4.10 M_bar   (fractional 33%)
    with a 10% coherent M_HSE floor: residual 3.09 +/- 0.20(stat) +/- 0.67(syst) -> 4.4 sigma, not 15
    DIRECTION MATTERS AND IT HELPS THE RECORD: L18 shows the ONE systematic that is actually
    measured (hydrostatic bias b in [0.00, 0.42]) moves the residual AWAY from zero, 3.09 -> 4.85
    at b = 0.20.  So the CONCLUSION (residual != 0) is robust; the NUMBER 13-15 sigma is not.

C2  A20 -- the binary-galaxy amplitudes.  This is the h9 pattern, in this lane's newest entry.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    L21's error bars come from ml_fit's profile likelihood (L21:417) -- statistics only.
    The amplitude A = observed/predicted with predicted sigma_los ~ (G M a0)^(1/4), so a COHERENT
    error in the K-band stellar M/L moves EVERY one of the 1900 pairs together:  dlnA = -(1/4) dlnUpsilon.
    L21's own systematics note says the shape axis 'is immune to this' -- i.e. the amplitude axis is not.
      Upsilon_K coherent at 0.10 dex -> dA = 0.104;  A = 1.802 +/- 0.041(stat) +/- 0.104(syst) -> 7.2 sigma  (quoted 19.6)
      Upsilon_K coherent at 0.15 dex -> dA = 0.156;  A = 1.802 +/- 0.041(stat) +/- 0.156(syst) -> 5.0 sigma  (quoted 19.6)
    AND the lane's own leading systematic is isolation depth: A falls 1.99 -> 1.51 as isolation
    deepens, a coherent one-sided 14% band the record itself calls decisive.
      combining stat + 0.10 dex M/L + the isolation band: A - 1 = 0.802 +/- 0.265 -> 3.0 sigma, not 19.6

    Same treatment for the ladder: A20's 'pairs need 30.9 +/- 1.6' has M_dyn/M_bar ~ 1/Upsilon,
      a 0.10 dex coherent Upsilon_K gives +/-8.0 on 30.9, so the gap to the cosmic 5.43 is
      3.1 sigma, not 16.  The factor 5.7 becomes 4.5-7.2.

C3  A7 -- '|z| = 13' (L2) and '12.8 sigma' (L6)
-----------------------------------------------
    These are z-scores between the cluster-required and galaxy-measured boost at matched acceleration
    (or matched density).  a0 cancels, correctly.  But the two populations do NOT share systematics,
    they have DIFFERENT ones, and neither is in the z: SPARC's Upsilon_disc = 0.5 / Upsilon_bulge = 0.7
    (coherent, ~0.11 dex in the literature) on one side, and the hydrostatic mass bias on the other.
    L18 prices the cluster side and finds it makes the gap WORSE (required b = -2.07 to close it).
    The SPARC side is not priced anywhere in this lane.  A 0.11 dex coherent Upsilon moves the
    galaxy boost by a factor 1.29 in g_bar, i.e. it cannot plausibly close a factor 2.2-5.1 --
    so the conclusion is safe, but '|z| = 13' and '12.8 sigma' remain statistics-only numbers.

C4  A22 -- the model case.  This is what the rest of section A should look like.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    A22 quotes 4.9/4.7 sigma AFTER a 0.227 dex coherent floor built from a named line-item budget
    (M/L+IMF 0.148, aperture/anisotropy 0.120, cluster model 0.088, instrument 0.052, estimator
    0.047, a0 footing 0.047, distance 0.021), and it states the stat-only 19.4 sigma as the thing
    being corrected.  Recomputed: sqrt(0.062^2 + 0.227^2) = 0.2353, 1.159/0.2353 = 4.93 sigma.

C5  A17 -- '6 sigma below' the abundance-matching prediction
------------------------------------------------------------
    L16-R4b: a0 returns only at log M_200 -1.80 dex, called '6.0 sigma below' LambdaCDM's prediction.
    The 0.30 dex denominator is the abundance-matching SPREAD (Moster vs Behroozi vs Kravtsov plus
    the 0.1 dex Upsilon systematic), i.e. a systematic used as a sigma. That is the right currency
    here -- it is a coherent modelling uncertainty, not a per-galaxy statistical error -- so this
    one is defensible. Recorded because it is the only sigma in section A built on a systematic.
  [FAIL] F3 [FAILURE MODE 3, correlated errors] every sigma and every error bar in section A either includes a coherent-systematic floor or is labelled statistics-only where it is quoted   (4 statistics-only significances presented without a floor: A8's 13-15 sigma (-> ~4 with a 10% coherent X-ray mass calibration), A20's whole amplitude table including 19.6 sigma (-> ~7 with a 0.10 dex Upsilon_K floor, ~3 including the lane's own isolation band) and its 16 sigma ladder gap (-> ~3), and A7's |z| = 13 / 12.8 sigma. In every case the DIRECTION of the conclusion survives and only the number falls. A22 is the counter-example and shows the lane knows how to do this)

